   median over subjects: T1 0.029  T2 0.028  T3 0.030 s
   -> cannot identify the event: T1, T2 and T3 are all locked to one another, so
      their intervals are the same. Subjects with |dIOI| >> 0.1 s (sub-08, sub-19)
      are timing anomalies between the official file and the PsychToolbox clock,
      not evidence about the anchor.

2. grand-average HbO locked to the official onset (all trials, all channels)
   sub-08   peak HbO at  +4.7 s
   sub-12   peak HbO at  +3.3 s
   sub-13   peak HbO at  +0.0 s
   sub-14   peak HbO at  +4.7 s
   sub-15   peak HbO at  +3.9 s
   sub-16   peak HbO at  +5.3 s
   sub-19   peak HbO at  +2.1 s
   sub-21   peak HbO at  +2.5 s
   sub-22   peak HbO at  +9.7 s
   sub-23   peak HbO at  +3.5 s
   sub-24   peak HbO at  +2.0 s
   sub-25   peak HbO at  +3.1 s
   grand average peak: +3.4 s;  per-subject median +3.4 s (IQR +2.4 to +4.7)
   -> peak at +3.4 s is too early to be a response to an event at 0, and
      fits a response to the cue at -3.19 s: FAVOURS onset = MOVIE
   caveat: the epoch ends at +12 s and peak latency varies by region and person;
   this is supporting evidence, weighed with test 1, not proof.

3. reproduction of Ning et al.'s published accuracy with their own pipeline
   onset = cue   3class:  53.14%  (published 49.4%, gap +3.74)
   onset = cue   2class:  75.23%  (published 78.3%, gap -3.07)
   onset = movie 3class:  37.04%  (published 49.4%, gap -12.36)
   onset = movie 2class:  55.42%  (published 78.3%, gap -22.88)
   -> total |gap|: cue 6.8 pp, movie 35.2 pp: their published result is reproduced only if onset = CUE.
   VERDICT (weighting test 3 over test 2): the official onset marks the CUE.
   Test 2's early peak is then a fast response to the cue itself, not a response to
   an earlier event; test 2 alone was misread as favouring the movie.

